00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.610 align:start position:0%
 
you  my specific job is supervising sound  editor and in some cases Co supervising  sound editor and I'm kind of usually  responsible for all the dialogue editing  all the ADR sort of all the words and  then in this in some cases I work with  Eugene Garrity who's more the sound  designer comes up with sound effects I  think to excel in sound editing mostly  you just have to want to do it and you  really have to be able to collaborate  our job essentially is to try to to get  the director to try to help the director  achieve his or her vision it's mostly  important to realize that most directors  will are thrilled if you come up with  something that they didn't think of or  wouldn't have thought of but primarily  you're there to serve the film and to  help tell the story the best way you can  usually I will get it sent a script  primarily to help do budgeting with the  film because if the film has a budget  which some don't have much of one it's  helpful to see on paper what you might  be faced with maybe get some ideas of  what might need to be recorded how long  it might take to do the job and also  just to get a feel for the film  generally as the film is being shot and  edited will get called upon to simply  furnish sound effects or occasionally if  there's a particularly bad sounding  dialogue scene to do a little bit of  work on it and maybe mix it a little bit  and then give stuff back to the editor  to use in their process for screenings  and then at a certain point when the  film is relatively close to being locked  they'll turn over the film and will  start the process of working on the  dialogue and  the sound effects and all the other  pieces and that's usually a eight to ten  week process then would go into the into  the dubbing stage into the mix for  anywhere from three to eight or ten  weeks again depending on the the size of  the film and then we eventually make a  print master and then do an  international track without the dialogue  and we're sort of the last ones usually  on the process good sound editing to me  depends completely on the type of film  that you're watching and listening to if  it's an action movie then there can be a  temptation to just keep piling on one  sound and after another it eventually  just becomes a cacophony you can't  there's no detail anymore I think an  action film benefits by having very  specific sounds that are good and clear  and do the job and then get out of that  one and move on into the next one if  it's an emotional scene in and suddenly  there's a a jet going by overhead it  might sort of take you out of the out of  the scene so I think on some films less  is more and on a narrative film I think  if you're not aware of the sound at all  and yet you're feeling all the emotion  involved then the sound editor did a  good job film that I that did inspire me  and this was after I had long been a  sound editor was Terminator 2 because  it's the kind of it's an action film it  doesn't become this big wash of sound  and the music complemented the sound  effects and vice versa and that to me  was a very good example of what could  have just been a lot of noise but was  very detailed Scorsese was in LA for  many years and he came back wanted to  come back and work in New York and he  was sort of looking for a new york-based  sound team I worked with a partner named  skip love say for several years and he  did a film called after hours  which was the first film that Marty did  in New York after coming back and I  think he was very pleased with the job  and I was started working with skip a  little bit after that and I guess my  first Scorsese job was Michael Jackson  video bad and that was about 1986 and  since then I've just kind of worked on  on everything and skip moved to Los  Angeles so wasn't really available to  continue so I sort of kept it up and  continued after he left I have a very  good relationship with Marty he we love  to make each other laugh so I'm sort of  the class clown on the mixed stage I  keep him happy he keeps us happy he's a  very charming man very funny  he is very specific about what he wants  it's very there's there's very little  question whether he likes something or  he doesn't he won't stop until it's the  way he wants it to be yet there's a lot  of room even in those places but  certainly throughout the rest of the  film to just experiment and do what you  want and he's always willing to listen  to something one of the greatest  challenges on Hugo in terms of the  dialogue and the production sound was  there's steam everywhere all through the  train station you see steam shooting out  of every vent or coming up through the  floor or down from the ceiling  all of those sequences were were very  noisy with the steam itself and the  machines that made the steam there were  lots of challenges for the production  sound mixer to try to cleanly mic the  actors and and that became my challenge  because Marty Scorsese and Thelma  Schoonmaker the editor get very it's all  about the emotion of the scene  technically they couldn't they do care  but they don't get bogged down in is  this like a perfectly pure take it if  take  is the one that they want it has the  right sound they don't care if a wrench  falls and clangs on the floor it's more  about getting the right emotional  quality from the scene so a lot of time  and effort was put into trying to get  the clean that clean up the production  sound to get rid of the steam as much as  possible  there were several microphones used in  in each scene radio mics and boom mics  and normally I try to use a boom mic  track and then sort of embellish it with  the radio mic only for the voice itself  and then kind of weave in and out of  that but in this case the boom tracks  were so noisy in most cases that we just  had to use a radio mic which means  picking up crackle and move whenever  anybody moves so it was a real challenge  just to try to clean up the tracks and  and get everything heard and get  everything to poke through the  additional sounds that that take place  in scenes where radio mics were used or  things where the dialogue was replaced  everything has to be added in but that's  kind of the case for everything all the  time anyway because eventually you have  to have an international track where the  dialogues the English dialogue is  removed and it's dubbed in one of many  languages so and pretty much everything  you see on the screen you have to have a  sound for eventually whether using the  production dialogue sounds or not my  favorite sequence in Hugo was one that I  did very little work on myself but my  partner Eugene Garrity did a great job  and that's sort of the the dream  sequence with the Train Hugo jumps down  on the track to try to pick up a key and  then the train's coming towards him and  it eventually goes off the track and  goes barreling through the station and  out the window  that's a good example I think of what I  was talking about before which is having  a lot of stuff going on  and hitting kind of one detail at a time  and having it build and build but not  some like a mush of of every sound  you've ever heard coming in at you all  at the same time  I think that's probably my favorite  sequence in that film

